# Decoding Human Brain Activity to Generate Multimedia Content to Visualize Thoughts for Patients with "Locked-in Syndrome"

### Project Overview

Locked-In Syndrome (LiS) is a rare and highly debilitating neurological condition in which the patient suffers from a complete paralysis of voluntary muscles except for those controlling eye movements. LiS can be caused by several factors, including stroke, traumatic head injuries, and neurodegenerative diseases such as amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS). Currently, there is no cure for LiS, and the treatment options are limited to supportive or rehabilitative care.

This project develops an ML-based communication system for Locked-in Syndrome patients using EEG and fMRI signals. After extracting neural patterns, a learning pipeline maps them to text, images, or speech outputs. By combining parallel signal processing with data-driven models, the system offers a non-invasive way to interpret brain activity for meaningful communication.

